---
import Callout from "@/components/Callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Playground from "@/components/Playground.astro";
import Sidenote from "@/components/Sidenote.astro";
import { guideGroups, guidePage } from "@/data/guide";
import Docs from "@/layouts/Docs.astro";
import Heading from "@/components/docs/Heading.astro";

const page = guidePage("/guide/getting-started");

const install = `npm install blotter.ts three`;

const imports = `import { Blotter, Material, Text } from "blotter.ts";
import { ChannelSplitMaterial } from "blotter.ts/materials";`;

const firstText = `import { Blotter, Material, Text } from "blotter.ts";

const text = new Text("observation", {
  family: "'Gloock', serif",
  size: 64,
  fill: "#171717",
  paddingLeft: 20,
  paddingRight: 20,
});

// The plain Material draws the text exactly as described.
const blotter = new Blotter(new Material(), { texts: text });

blotter.forText(text)?.appendTo(output);
await blotter.ready;`;

const fonts = `const text = new Text("observation", {
  family: "'Gloock', serif",
  size: 64,
});

// Load the exact face, weight and size Blotter is about to draw with.
await document.fonts.load('400 64px "Gloock"', text.value);
await document.fonts.ready;

const blotter = new Blotter(material, { texts: text });`;
---

<Docs
  title="Getting started · blotter.ts"
  description={page.description}
  current="guide"
  eyebrow="Guide"
  heading={page.title}
  lead={page.description}
  groups={guideGroups()}
  sections={page.sections}
>
  <Heading id="why-blotter">Why Blotter</Heading>
  <p>
    CSS runs out of tricks for text quite quickly. You get shadows, gradients, a clip-path if
    you are feeling brave, and then you are exporting PNGs. Blotter takes a different route: it
    hands each word on your page to a fragment shader and draws it, every frame, on the GPU.
    The text stays typographic. Your font, your size, your weight. Only the pixels are up for
    negotiation.
  </p>
  <p>
    The trade is that what comes back is a <code>canvas</code>, not a DOM text node. Nobody
    can select it and screen readers will not see it, so keep the real text nearby. Blotter is
    for titles, headings, logotypes and words used as graphics. It is the wrong tool for a
    paragraph.
  </p>

  <Heading id="install">Install</Heading>
  <p>
    Blotter renders through <code>three</code> and leaves it to you to install, so the two go
    in together.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={install} lang="sh" />
  <p>
    The package is an ES module with types. Everything comes from two entry points: the core
    classes from <code>blotter.ts</code>, and the ready-made effects from
    <code>blotter.ts/materials</code>. Import what you use; the rest is shaken out of your
    bundle.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={imports} lang="ts" />

  <Heading id="first-text">Your first text</Heading>
  <p>
    A Blotter needs two things: at least one <code>Text</code>, and a <code>Material</code>
    that says how to draw it. The plain <code>Material</code> applies no effect at all, which
    makes it the right place to start: if the word below looks like the word you typed, the
    pipeline is working.
  </p>
  <Sidenote>
    The examples on this page run as you type. <code>output</code> is the box under each
    editor.
  </Sidenote>
  <Playground id="first-text" code={firstText} />

  <Heading id="what-happened">What just happened</Heading>
  <p>
    Four objects took part, and you will meet them again on every page of this guide.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A <strong>Text</strong> is a string plus the styles to draw it with. Blotter measures it and
      rasterises it once, at construction.
    </li>
    <li>
      A <strong>Material</strong> is a fragment shader with named uniforms. Every text that shares
      a material is drawn by the same program.
    </li>
    <li>
      The <strong>Blotter</strong> packs its texts into one texture atlas, renders the whole atlas
      through the material in a single WebGL draw call, and keeps doing so on
      <code>requestAnimationFrame</code>. There is one WebGL context for the entire page, however
      many Blotters you create.
    </li>
    <li>
      A <strong>RenderScope</strong> is what you get back from <code>blotter.forText(text)</code>: a
      per-text <code>canvas</code> that copies its own rectangle out of the shared buffer each
      frame, plus a place to set uniforms for this text alone.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <code>blotter.ready</code> resolves after the first build. Nothing needs it to be awaited
    before appending a canvas, but anything that reads per-text uniforms or the rendered
    <code>imageData</code> should wait for it.
  </p>

  <Heading id="fonts">Fonts come first</Heading>
  <p>
    Because a text is rasterised the moment it is constructed, the font has to be there
    already. Build a Blotter while a web font is still downloading and you will get the
    fallback face baked into the atlas. The fix is one line: ask the font loading API for the
    exact face, weight and size you are about to use.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={fonts} lang="ts" />
  <Callout title="Why the exact size?">
    <p>
      <code>document.fonts.load()</code> takes a CSS <code>font</code> shorthand and only
      guarantees the faces that match it. Variable fonts with optical sizes, and families with
      separate files per weight, both need the real numbers.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <Heading id="where-next">Where next</Heading>
  <p>
    <a href="/guide/texts-and-uniforms">Texts and uniforms</a> covers styling, changing a text
    after the fact, and turning the dials on a running effect. The
    <a href="/materials">materials</a> pages show the five bundled effects with live controls.
    When none of them is quite right, <a href="/guide/custom-materials">custom materials</a>
    walks through writing your own, and the <a href="/api">API reference</a> lists everything
    the package exports.
  </p>
</Docs>
